      en: "Cahuita National Park, established on the Caribbean coast in 1970, protects 1,100 hectares of land and a 22,400-hectare marine area. Its coral reefs are among the healthiest in Costa Rica, supporting vibrant marine life. The park's white-sand beaches, including Playa Blanca, are critical nesting sites for hawksbill and green turtles. Inland, the rainforest shelters howler monkeys, sloths, and toucans. The 8-km Sendero Cahuita trail winds along the coast through mangroves and palm groves. The park is co-managed with the local indigenous community, promoting sustainable tourism.",
